Let $n \geq 3$ and let $S$ be an $n$-simplex in Euclidean space $\mathbb{E}^n$, that is, the convex hull of $n+1$ affinely independent points. Define:
- the centroid $G$ of $S$ as the arithmetic mean of its $n+1$ vertices;
- the circumcenter $C$ of $S$ as the unique point equidistant from all $n+1$ vertices (the center of the circumscribed sphere);
- the incenter $I$ of $S$ as the unique point in the interior of $S$ equidistant from all $n+1$ facet hyperplanes (the center of the inscribed sphere);
- the Euler line of $S$ as the affine hull of $C$ and $G$ (a line, provided $C \neq G$).

Give a purely geometric characterization of those $n$-simplices $S \subset \mathbb{E}^n$ (for each $n \geq 3$) for which the incenter $I$ lies on the Euler line of $S$.

(For comparison, in the planar case $n = 2$, a triangle has its incenter on its Euler line if and only if it is isosceles; the problem asks for the analogous characterization in dimension $n \geq 3$.)

